/**
 * @defgroup optimal_motor_shaft_power_calculator Optimal Motor Shaft Power Calculator
 * @ingroup equipment_and_property_calculators
 * @brief Calculates motor mechanical output required to supply a pump or fan through a drive.
 * @details
 * How much motor shaft output is required to supply a known pump or fan shaft-power duty?
 * A belt transmits mechanical power and dissipates some of it before it reaches the mover.
 * Accounting for that transmission loss connects the mover's required shaft input to the
 * mechanical output the motor must supply. "Optimal" describes the supplied duty; this
 * calculation does not search for an optimum, select a motor size or check a motor rating.
 *
 * @heading{Final Results and Interpretation}
 * The results are required motor shaft output in hp and drive efficiency as a fraction.
 * For belt drives, add estimated transmission loss to the supplied mover shaft requirement:
 * @formula{optimal-motor-shaft-power-results;
 * P_m=P_s+P_{loss},\qquad \eta_d=\frac{1}{1+\ell}
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{P_m; Required motor mechanical shaft output entering the drive; \horsepower}
 * @symrow{P_s; Required mechanical input at the pump or fan shaft; \horsepower}
 * @symrow{P_{loss}; Estimated power dissipated by the belt drive; \horsepower}
 * @symrow{\ell; Belt loss relative to mover shaft power; \unitless}
 * @symrow{\eta_d; Fraction of motor shaft output delivered to the mover; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * At positive shaft power with ordinary belt losses, the motor must supply more mechanical
 * power than the mover requires. An efficiency of 0.95 means 95% of motor shaft output reaches
 * the mover. These results exclude motor electrical losses and do not represent electrical
 * demand or fluid power. The
 * @ref optimal_pump_shaft_power_calculator "Optimal Pump Shaft Power Calculator" supplies
 * pump/fan shaft requirements from a duty and supplied efficiency; the
 * @ref optimal_motor_power_calculator "Optimal Motor Power Calculator" estimates electrical
 * demand upstream of the resulting motor shaft requirement.
 *
 * @heading{Choose the Drive Path}
 * Supply the required mover shaft power in hp, the drive selection and specified efficiency
 * as a fraction. All fields are required; specified efficiency is used only for SPECIFIED.
 * Direct drive assumes no transmission loss:
 * @formula{optimal-motor-shaft-power-direct; P_m=P_s,\qquad \eta_d=1}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{P_m; Required motor shaft output; \horsepower}
 * @symrow{P_s; Supplied mover shaft requirement; \horsepower}
 * @symrow{\eta_d; Direct-drive efficiency; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * When a drive efficiency is known, SPECIFIED uses that fraction directly:
 * @formula{optimal-motor-shaft-power-specified;
 * P_m=\frac{P_s}{\eta_{specified}},\qquad \eta_d=\eta_{specified}
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{P_m; Required motor shaft output; \horsepower}
 * @symrow{P_s; Supplied mover shaft requirement; \horsepower}
 * @symrow{\eta_{specified}; Supplied drive efficiency fraction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{\eta_d; Returned drive efficiency; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * Ordinary V-belt, notched V-belt and synchronous-belt drives instead use the empirical
 * power-dependent model below. Unknown C++ drive values retain the ordinary V-belt fallback;
 * this does not add supported values to the public WASM Drive enum.
 *
 * @heading{Estimate Belt Transmission Loss}
 * Evaluate the retained ordinary V-belt curve at the mover shaft power, not motor output:
 * @formula{optimal-motor-shaft-power-belt-curve;
 * C(P_s)=a\exp(-bP_s)+c\exp(-dP_s)+e
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{C; Numerical empirical loss-curve value in percentage points; \percent}
 * @symrow{P_s; Mover shaft power at which the curve is evaluated; \horsepower}
 * @symrow{a,c,e; Retained coefficients 0.7489574, 4.136368 and 4.162707; \percent}
 * @symrow{b,d; Retained decay coefficients 0.02067997 and 0.226025; \per\horsepower}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * Convert the curve to a fraction and apply the selected belt's relative loss multiplier:
 * @formula{optimal-motor-shaft-power-belt-loss;
 * \ell=\frac{C(P_s)f}{100},\qquad P_{loss}=\ell P_s
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{\ell; Belt loss relative to mover shaft power; \unitless}
 * @symrow{C; Numerical empirical curve value in percentage points; \percent}
 * @symrow{P_s; Required mover shaft input; \horsepower}
 * @symrow{f; Relative loss multiplier: 1 for ordinary, 0.05/0.07 for notched, 0.02/0.07 for synchronous; \unitless}
 * @symrow{100; Numerical percentage-point-to-fraction divisor; \unitless}
 * @symrow{P_{loss}; Estimated belt transmission loss; \horsepower}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * The reference fractions 0.07, 0.05 and 0.02 scale losses. Their historical background uses
 * nominal efficiencies of 93%, 95% and 98%; these are not fixed efficiencies returned here.
 * Because loss is expressed relative to mover power, efficiency is one divided by one plus
 * the loss fraction. It is not one minus that fraction.
 *
 * Arithmetic retains the model's evaluation order: for scaled belts, multiply the curve by
 * the relative factor before dividing by 100. Ordinary V-belt divides its unscaled curve by 100.
 * Multiply the fraction by mover power, then add the resulting hp loss. Motor output is
 * computed by this addition rather than division by returned efficiency. Replacing these
 * expressions with algebraic equivalents can change floating-point boundary outcomes.
 *
 * @heading{Worked Notched V-Belt Example}
 * A pump or fan requires 50 hp at its shaft and uses a notched V-belt. The unscaled curve is
 * approximately 4.4291 percentage points. Applying the notched multiplier gives:
 * @formula{optimal-motor-shaft-power-example-loss;
 * \ell\approx\frac{4.4291(0.05/0.07)}{100}\approx0.03164,\qquad
 * P_{loss}\approx0.03164(50)\approx1.582\ \mathrm{hp}
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{\ell; Notched-belt loss fraction relative to mover input; \unitless}
 * @symrow{P_{loss}; Mechanical transmission loss; \horsepower}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * Add that loss to the required mover input and calculate the drive efficiency:
 * @formula{optimal-motor-shaft-power-example-result;
 * P_m\approx50+1.582\approx51.58\ \mathrm{hp},\qquad
 * \eta_d\approx\frac{1}{1+0.03164}\approx0.9693
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{P_m; Required motor shaft output before the drive; \horsepower}
 * @symrow{\eta_d; Returned drive efficiency, approximately 96.93 percent; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * About 51.58 hp of motor mechanical output supplies 50 hp to the mover. This value must
 * still be passed through a motor-efficiency calculation to estimate electrical demand.
 * For comparison, direct drive requires 50 hp and returns efficiency 1.
 *
 * @heading{Worked Specified-Efficiency Example}
 * If measured or otherwise appropriate drive efficiency is 0.95, use SPECIFIED instead:
 * @formula{optimal-motor-shaft-power-example-specified;
 * P_m=\frac{50}{0.95}\approx52.63\ \mathrm{hp},\qquad \eta_d=0.95
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{P_m; Required motor shaft output for a 50 hp mover duty; \horsepower}
 * @symrow{\eta_d; Supplied and returned drive efficiency; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * The 95% efficiency describes the assumed drive at this operating point; it does not select
 * the notched or synchronous empirical model. The caller must supply a fraction, not 95.
 *
 * @heading{Physical Limits and Retained Arithmetic}
 * Physically meaningful consuming duties use positive power and a drive efficiency fraction
 * greater than zero and no greater than one. The repository establishes no validated power
 * range for the empirical fit. Callers select appropriate inputs and convert user-facing units.
 * No validation, clamping or substitution of defaults is performed.
 *
 * At zero mover power, belt loss and required motor output are zero, while the reciprocal
 * expression returns a finite belt efficiency. This is the curve's zero-duty arithmetic,
 * not a measured no-load efficiency. Direct drive returns efficiency 1; SPECIFIED returns
 * the supplied fraction. A zero specified efficiency with nonzero power produces a signed
 * infinity; zero power divided by zero produces NaN. Negative or above-one efficiencies,
 * negative powers, signed zeros, NaN, infinities, underflow and overflow retain raw arithmetic.
 * Large negative powers can overflow the growing exponential terms. These outcomes are not
 * validated physical predictions.
 *
 * The @ref mover_shaft_power_calculator "Mover Shaft Power Calculator" starts with motor output
 * and estimates power delivered to the mover using a different empirical curve. The two belt
 * models are not exact algebraic inverses; a forward/reverse round trip is not an identity.
 * Do not apply drive losses twice or treat this calculation as motor selection, optimization,
 * motor electrical efficiency or pump/fan hydraulic efficiency.
 *
 * @heading{Provenance}
 * The retained reverse-direction source points to the forward MoverShaftPower model for
 * background. Historical comments in that model attribute its belt-loss relationship to
 * AMCA 203-90 Appendix L and relative belt factors to a Belt Drive Tip Sheet. The repository
 * does not establish the derivation of this reverse model's distinct coefficients, their
 * validated range or a verified external source for this exact fit. The values are retained
 * model coefficients, not newly derived constants or evidence of standards compliance.
 *
 * @heading{Current Calculation Interface}
 * C++ callers use optimal_motor_shaft_power::calculate with a complete Input and receive a
 * Result. WASM/TypeScript callers use calculateOptimalMotorShaftPower with moverShaftPower,
 * drive and specifiedEfficiency, receiving motorShaftPower and driveEfficiency. Power is in
 * hp and efficiency is dimensionless. Inputs and results are plain values requiring no cleanup.
 */
